  "toc.title": "Contents",
  "common.replay": "Replay",

  "hero.kicker": "Chemistry · Chemical Reactions · An Interactive Essay",
  "hero.title": "Why Batteries Just Can't Hold More Charge",
  "hero.sub": "Phones charge once a day, electric cars have to stop and charge every few hundred kilometres,<br>and charging always slows down toward the end",
  "hero.lead": "Over the past thirty-odd years, the energy a lithium battery stores per kilogram has only a bit more than doubled, while the price of a cell fell to about 1% of its 1991 value by 2023. Why does energy rise so slowly? Why does the last stretch of fast charging drag? Why do batteries catch fire and wear out? This essay starts from two half-reactions and builds up one level at a time: <strong>chemistry sets the ceiling, diffusion and resistance set the speed, heat and side reactions set how safe and how long-lived a cell is, and production volume sets the price.</strong> Each chapter has a model you can push around, using the equations this field actually uses, scaled down. After reading, you should be able to tell which numbers in a battery news story matter, and which claims nobody has verified yet.",
  "hero.note": "This page was written by an AI. The models are scaled-down versions of standard models from textbooks and papers; the sources are given in each chapter and in the footer. Some parameters are chosen so that the shapes look right, and the page says so beside them. <strong>A number a model computes is the model's output, not a measurement of any particular battery</strong>; numbers marked “measured” or with a citation come from the literature. Each fact in the frontier chapter is dated, and the facts will go out of date. This page is not investment or purchase advice.",

  /* ───── 01 ───── */
  "ch1.title": "Electrons Are Forced Around a Detour",
  "ch1.law": "The voltage of a battery is the difference in how eagerly the two half-reactions want electrons; the energy each electron carries equals that voltage.",
  "ch1.p1": "Put a piece of zinc into copper sulfate solution, and copper deposits directly onto the zinc and the solution heats up: the zinc hands its electrons to the copper ions, and the energy turns into heat. A battery does one thing: <strong>it keeps the two half-reactions apart</strong>. Zinc loses electrons on one side (<em>Zn → Zn²⁺ + 2e⁻</em>), and copper ions gain electrons on the other (<em>Cu²⁺ + 2e⁻ → Cu</em>). Electrons cannot pass through the solution in the middle, so they must take the external wire, powering your light bulb or phone on the way. Ions move inside and electrons move outside; both paths must be open at the same time for the reaction to run. How a galvanic cell is put together and which way the electrons flow is shown step by step on the site's <a href=\"../electrochemistry/#step0\">Electrochemistry</a> page, so it is not repeated here.",
  "ch1.p2": "Each half-reaction has a <strong>standard electrode potential</strong> E°, measured against the same reference (the standard hydrogen electrode). The more negative the potential, the more readily the half-reaction gives up electrons; the more positive, the more readily it accepts them. Pair two half-reactions, and the cell voltage is the difference between them. Voltage times charge is energy, so the maximum work per mole of electrons is:",
  "ch1.eq": "E<sub>cell</sub> = E°<sub>cathode</sub> − E°<sub>anode</sub>&emsp;&emsp;ΔG = −nFE&emsp;(F = 96 485 C/mol, the Faraday constant)",
  "ch1.model.title": "Potential ladder: pick two half-reactions and read the drop between them",
  "ch1.model.how": "The vertical axis is potential against the standard hydrogen electrode. Pick an anode (the side that gives up electrons) and a cathode (the side that accepts them); the gap between them is the cell voltage. The blue band is the range where water is stable at neutral (pH 7). The water-couple values in the table are standard-state (acidic) values: an anode below the band's lower edge can reduce water to hydrogen, and a cathode above its upper edge can oxidize water to oxygen.",
  "ch1.model.aria": "Potential ladder: electrode potentials arranged in a column; the gap between the chosen anode and cathode is the cell voltage, and the stable window of water is marked",
  "ch1.leg.anode": "Anode",
  "ch1.leg.cathode": "Cathode",
  "ch1.leg.water": "Water stability window (pH 7)",
  "ch1.ctl.anode": "Anode",
