  "ch1.unit.V": "{v} V",
  "ch1.unit.kJ": "{v} kJ",
  "ch1.unit.eV": "{v} eV",
  "ch1.say.same": "The anode and the cathode picked the same half-reaction, so there is no gap: the voltage is 0 and nothing happens.",
  "ch1.say.rev": "The “anode” you picked sits <em>{E} V</em> above the “cathode”, so electrons would run the other way: if you really connected a wire, {a} would be the side that accepts electrons. Try swapping the two choices.",
  "ch1.say.inside": "{a} with {c}: voltage <em>{E} V</em>, at most {G} kJ of work per mole of electrons. Both electrodes lie inside water's stability window, so using water as the electrolyte is fine, but the voltage tops out here.",
  "ch1.say.low": "{a} with {c}: voltage <em>{E} V</em>. The anode lies below water's window, so thermodynamically it can reduce water to hydrogen. Whether that is usable depends on how slow this reaction is at the electrode surface.",
  "ch1.say.high": "{a} with {c}: voltage <em>{E} V</em>. The cathode lies above water's window, so thermodynamically it can oxidize water to oxygen. Whether that is usable depends on how slow this reaction is at the electrode surface.",
  "ch1.say.both": "{a} with {c}: voltage <em>{E} V</em>, {G} kJ per mole of electrons, far above water's 1.23 V window, so both sides would split water. This pair can only work in a water-free organic electrolyte.",
  "ch1.p3": "Push the ladder around and three things show up. The classic zinc–copper cell is only <strong>1.10 V</strong>, doing 106 kJ of work per mole of electrons. The lead–acid battery in a car is <strong>2.04 V</strong>, already above water's 1.23 V window: it works because hydrogen and oxygen evolution on lead are very slow, a kinetic fluke, and at the end of charging it still gasses. Lithium sits at the very bottom of the table (−3.04 V), and paired with lithium cobalt oxide it gives <strong>3.90 V</strong>, 376 kJ per mole of electrons, more than three times the zinc–copper value.",
  "ch1.p4": "The cost is that voltages this high cannot be held by any water-based electrolyte: the cell must use carbonate organic solvents with a lithium salt. Organic solvents burn, which sets up chapter 6. Two more details recur later on. First, a real lithium-ion negative electrode is not lithium metal but graphite with lithium inserted; on average it sits only 0.18 V above lithium, so it loses almost no voltage. Second, the positive electrode does not have a single potential either, but a curve that changes with how much lithium it holds; chapter 3 covers this.",
  "ch1.limit": "Limits of the model: for the water-based couples in the table, the values are standard electrode potentials (OpenStax Chemistry 2e, Appendix L). The “potential” of an intercalation electrode is its average over the working range, computed from the measured open-circuit voltage curves in chapter 3 and converted to the hydrogen scale with Li⁺/Li = −3.04 V. Lithium cobalt oxide is taken as about 3.9 V (against lithium). ΔG = −nFE is the reversible limit; in a real discharge some energy always turns into heat, which chapter 5 calculates. The water window is drawn at its neutral (pH 7) position, while the H and O couples in the table are acidic standard-state values, so they do not land exactly on the band's two edges. As pH changes, the band's upper and lower edges shift together (about 59 mV per pH unit); its width stays 1.23 V. The window in lead–acid sulfuric acid sits higher overall, but still cannot fit the 2.04 V.",

  /* ───── 02 ───── */
  "ch2.title": "Why Lithium, and the Mass That Does Nothing",
  "ch2.law": "How much charge an electrode can store per gram is set by the Faraday constant divided by the molar mass; and in a real cell, only a little over half of the material actually takes part in the reaction.",
  "ch2.p1": "How much charge a battery can store depends first on one thing: how much material has to be carried along for each electron exchanged. One mole of electrons carries 96 485 coulombs, which in everyday units is 26.8 Ah. So the <strong>theoretical specific capacity</strong> of a material, per gram, is:",
  "ch2.eq": "Q = nF / (3.6·M)&emsp;mAh/g&emsp;&emsp;(n: electrons exchanged per formula unit, M: molar mass in g/mol)",
  "ch2.p2": "Lithium's molar mass is only 6.94, which gives <strong>3862 mAh/g</strong>, higher than any anode material in practical use, and its potential is the most negative on the whole table. Six carbon atoms in graphite hold one lithium (LiC₆), giving only 372 mAh/g; one silicon atom can hold 3.75 lithium atoms (Li₁₅Si₄), giving 3579 mAh/g, close to lithium itself. The positive electrodes are much heavier: 274 mAh/g for lithium cobalt oxide, 276 for NMC811 and 170 for LFP, because each lithium has to be carried along with a transition-metal atom and two to four oxygen atoms. In a cell the two electrodes are connected in series, so the charge has to pass through both, and the total capacity is found by adding reciprocals (1/Q = 1/Q<sub>cathode</sub> + 1/Q<sub>anode</sub>); multiplying by the average voltage gives the theoretical specific energy. The model below starts from this theoretical value and adds, one by one, the things a real cell cannot do without.",
  "ch2.model.title": "From theoretical value to a cell: how much each step costs",
  "ch2.model.how": "Each row is one step: first convert to the capacity the material can reversibly insert and extract, then add the binder and conductive additive, the extra negative-electrode margin, the aluminum and copper foils, the separator, the electrolyte, and finally the can, cap and insulators. Areal capacity is the capacity per square centimetre of electrode: the thicker the coating, the less foil and separator per Ah. The green band is the range for mass-produced cells given by Frith et al. (2023) (NMC/NCA small cylindrical cells 245–270 Wh/kg, LFP 160–200 Wh/kg; LCO is not drawn); the red dashed line is a 4680 cell measured by teardown.",
  "ch2.model.aria": "Waterfall chart: starting from the theoretical specific energy, subtract each inactive mass item in turn to get the cell's specific energy",
  "ch2.leg.step": "Specific energy after each step",
  "ch2.leg.real": "Mass-produced cell range (Frith et al. 2023)",
  "ch2.leg.4680": "Tesla 4680 measured (Ank et al. 2023)",
  "ch2.ctl.cathode": "Cathode",
  "ch2.ctl.anode": "Anode",
  "ch2.ctl.areal": "Areal capacity",
