  "ch4.cv.x": "From centre to surface r/R",
  "ch4.cv.y": "Lithium fraction",
  "ch4.say.run": "Charging…",
  "ch4.say.full": "At {c}, this particle fills completely. Its diffusion time τ is {tau}, shorter than the {tf} needed to fill it, so the inside keeps up with the surface.",
  "ch4.say.fullslow": "At {c}, this particle still fills completely, even though its diffusion time τ ({tau}) is longer than the {tf} this C-rate gives. τ only sets the order of magnitude: the surface has to run well ahead of the average before it reaches 1, and here it has not yet. Speed up a little more and it will not fill.",
  "ch4.say.cut": "At {c}, the surface is full after {t}, and the particle takes in only <em>{fill}%</em>. The diffusion time τ is {tau}, while this C-rate allows only {tf}: the inside cannot keep up.",
  "ch4.p3": "The graphite particles in LG M50 have a radius of about 5.9 µm, so τ = 17 minutes; in this single-particle model, at 10C (the rate that fills in six minutes) it still takes in 92%. NMC811 particles are smaller, but diffusion is much slower (D is eight times smaller), so τ is close to 1.9 hours: at 2C it takes in 89%, and at 4C only 68%. LFP's diffusion coefficient is thousands of times smaller than graphite's, so on D alone it should be the slowest. In industry the fix is to make the particles tens of nanometres across: shrinking the radius by more than a hundredfold cuts the time by more than ten thousandfold, so τ is only 7 minutes.",
  "ch4.p4": "This is the most common trade-off in battery design: the smaller the particles and the thinner the electrodes, the faster the charging, but the more surface there is for side reactions (chapter 7), and the harder it is to pack particles densely, so less is stored per unit volume (chapter 2). Fast-charging and long-range cells often use the same chemistry; the difference lies in these dimensions.",
  "ch4.limit": "Limits of the model: a single spherical particle, a constant diffusion coefficient and a constant surface flux; the concentration gradient in the electrolyte and the particle-size distribution are not computed. The diffusion coefficient of real graphite changes strongly with lithium content, and values in the literature can differ by several orders of magnitude. LFP is a two-phase reaction, so describing it with single-phase diffusion is only an approximation. The fill fraction here is an upper limit for one particle; the next chapter puts it into a whole cell.",

  /* ───── 05 ───── */
  "ch5.title": "Why the Last 20% Is So Slow",
  "ch5.law": "The larger the current, the further the terminal voltage strays from the open-circuit voltage. The charger can only watch the upper voltage limit, so the closer to full, the smaller the current.",
  "ch5.p1": "A real battery can be treated as an open-circuit voltage that changes with charge, in series with several kinds of “resistance”. There is the ohmic resistance of the electrolyte and current collectors (a voltage loss IR; step 4 of the <a href=\"../../../physics/circuits/ohms-law/#step4\">Ohm’s Law</a> page covers the internal resistance of a cell). There is the extra push a reaction needs at the electrode surface, called overpotential, described by the Butler–Volmer equation. And there is the diffusion from the previous chapter: the surface of a particle fills faster than its interior, so the surface potential reaches the limit first. While charging:",
  "ch5.eq": "V = U<sub>cathode</sub>(y<sub>surface</sub>) − U<sub>anode</sub>(x<sub>surface</sub>) + η<sub>cathode</sub> + η<sub>anode</sub> + I·R&emsp;&emsp;η = (2RT/F)·arsinh( j / 2j₀ )",
  "ch5.p2": "Lithium-ion charging is usually constant current–constant voltage (CC–CV): first charge at a fixed current until the terminal voltage reaches the upper limit (here 4.2 V); then hold 4.2 V and let the current fall by itself until it drops to C/20, which counts as full. The larger the current, the bigger every loss, and the sooner the terminal voltage reaches 4.2 V; the constant-current stage then ends earlier, and the rest is a slow grind in the constant-voltage stage.",
  "ch5.model.title": "Single-particle model: constant-current constant-voltage charging of a 5 Ah cell",
  "ch5.model.how": "This is the “single particle model” (SPM) widely used in battery research: the positive and negative electrodes are each represented by one typical particle, diffusion is solved inside each particle, the surface uses Butler–Volmer kinetics, and an ohmic resistance is put in series. Parameters come from the LG M50 cell (Chen et al. 2020), and the diffusion and reaction rates vary with temperature following the Arrhenius relation. The upper plot shows state of charge and current; the lower plot shows the terminal voltage. The particle-size slider scales the radii of both particles together.",
  "ch5.model.aria": "Constant-current constant-voltage charging: state of charge rises over time, the current falls gradually during the constant-voltage stage, and the terminal voltage rises to 4.2 V and then holds",
